This usually happens because the parent component re-renders and passes new object or function references, even if the values look identical.

Here are the most common culprits:

**Objects and arrays created in the render method** — every render creates a new reference, so React thinks the prop changed even though the values are the same. Move them outside the component or use `useMemo`.

```jsx
// ❌ New object every render
<Child config={{color: 'red', size: 10}} />

// ✅ Reference stays the same
const config = {color: 'red', size: 10};
<Child config={config} />
```

**Functions created inline** — same issue as objects. Use `useCallback` to keep the same function reference across renders.

```jsx
// ❌ New function every render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />

// ✅ Same function reference
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

**The parent re-renders** — if the parent component re-renders, all its children re-render by default. Wrap the child in `React.memo` to skip re-renders when props are the same.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ prop }) => <div>{prop}</div>);
```

The parent is usually re-rendering because its own state changed or a context it depends on changed. You can see this in React DevTools — check the "Highlight updates" option to watch which components re-render.
